  ${
    $d A u $.  $d C x $.  $d E u $.  $d K u x $.  $d L u x $.  $d X u x $.
    $d Y u x $.  $d ph u x $.
    itgsubsticc.1 $e |- ( ph -> X e. RR ) $.
    itgsubsticc.2 $e |- ( ph -> Y e. RR ) $.
    itgsubsticc.3 $e |- ( ph -> X <_ Y ) $.
    itgsubsticc.4 $e |- ( ph -> ( x e. ( X [,] Y ) |-> A ) e. ( ( X [,] Y )
       -cn-> ( K [,] L ) ) ) $.
    itgsubsticc.5 $e |- ( ph -> ( u e. ( K [,] L ) |-> C ) e. ( ( K [,] L )
       -cn-> CC ) ) $.
    itgsubsticc.6 $e |- ( ph -> ( x e. ( X (,) Y ) |-> B ) e. ( ( ( X (,) Y )
       -cn-> CC ) i^i L^1 ) ) $.
    itgsubsticc.7 $e |- ( ph -> ( RR _D ( x e. ( X [,] Y ) |-> A ) ) = ( x e. (
       X (,) Y ) |-> B ) ) $.
    itgsubsticc.8 $e |- ( u = A -> C = E ) $.
    itgsubsticc.9 $e |- ( x = X -> A = K ) $.
    itgsubsticc.10 $e |- ( x = Y -> A = L ) $.
    itgsubsticc.11 $e |- ( ph -> K e. RR ) $.
    itgsubsticc.12 $e |- ( ph -> L e. RR ) $.
    $( Integration by u-substitution.  The main difference with respect to
       ~ itgsubst is that here we consider the range of ` A ( x ) ` to be in
       the closed interval ` ( K [,] L ) ` .  If ` A ( x ) ` is a continuous,
       differentiable function from ` [ X , Y ] ` to ` ( Z , W ) ` , whose
       derivative is continuous and integrable, and ` C ( u ) ` is a continuous
       function on ` ( Z , W ) ` , then the integral of ` C ( u ) ` from
       ` K = A ( X ) ` to ` L = A ( Y ) ` is equal to the integral of
       ` C ( A ( x ) ) _D A ( x ) ` from ` X ` to ` Y ` .  (Contributed by
       Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) $)
    itgsubsticc $p |- ( ph -> S_ [ K -> L ] C _d u = S_ [ X -> Y ] ( E x. B )
       _d x ) $=
      ( cicc co cmpt cr wcel cfv clt wbr cif eqid cle w3a eqidd wceq adantl cxr
      cv rexrd ubicc2 syl3anc fvmptd ccncf cncff syl ffvelcdmd eqeltrrd syl2anc
      wf wb elicc2 mpbid simp2d itgsubsticclem ) ABCDEFGCHIUDUEZFUFZCUGCUTZVQUH
